<map version="1.0.1">
<node TEXT="Design of Intention Recognition Layer"><node TEXT="first step"><node TEXT="features"><node TEXT="~ 20 subtasks"></node><node TEXT="deal with fuzzy match, typo correction, etc"></node></node><node TEXT="how to do"><node TEXT="helper tasks" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="normalization and clean"><node TEXT=""></node></node><node TEXT="&quot;Autocorrect&quot; (Fuzzy &amp; Typo Matching)"><node TEXT=""></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Linguist&quot; (Semantic &amp; Domain Prep)"><node TEXT=""></node></node></node><node TEXT="why use simple helper tasks" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="1. Does it beat LLM in Accuracy?"><node TEXT="Yes, for specific routing."></node><node TEXT="LLMs are probabilistic. If you ask an LLM to &quot;Pick one of these 100 tasks,&quot; it might pick Task #12 today and Task #13 tomorrow for the exact same input because of &quot;temperature&quot; or slight prompt variations."></node><node TEXT="Simple Algorithms (Regex/Fuzzy): Are Deterministic. If it matches, it matches 100% of the time. There is no &quot;hallucination&quot; in a lookup table."></node><node TEXT="LLM: Even GPT-4o has a 2-5% error rate on complex routing with many options. At 1,000,000 queries, that’s 50,000 broken experiences."></node></node><node TEXT="2. Does it beat LLM in Performance?"><node TEXT="Yes, by orders of magnitude."></node><node TEXT="LLM Route: Requires sending tokens over the internet, waiting for the model to &quot;think&quot; (inference), and streaming the result back."><node TEXT="Latency: 500ms – 2s"></node><node TEXT="Cost: $0.01 - $0.05 per call."></node></node><node TEXT="Simple Algorithm Route: Happens locally on your CPU in memory."><node TEXT="Latency: &lt;1ms – 10ms"></node><node TEXT="Cost: $0.000001 (basically free)."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Where the Complexity pays for itself"><node TEXT="A. The &quot;Cold Start&quot; Problem"><node TEXT="If you have 100 tasks, an LLM prompt containing all 100 descriptions will be massive (10k+ tokens). This makes every single &quot;Hello&quot; from a user expensive. A modular intention layer only &quot;wakes up&quot; the specific task logic needed, keeping the main loop lean."></node></node><node TEXT="B. Debugging (The &quot;Black Box&quot; vs. &quot;Glass Box&quot;)"><node TEXT="Pure LLM: If it routes a refund request to a sales agent, you have to &quot;vibe-check&quot; the prompt and hope it doesn&apos;t break something else."></node><node TEXT="Modular System: You can see exactly where it failed. &quot;The Fuzzy Matcher failed to catch the typo, and the Semantic Router gave it a 0.5 score.&quot; You can fix that specific logic gate without touching the rest of the system."></node></node><node TEXT="C. Scaling to 100+ Tasks"><node TEXT="An LLM&apos;s performance degrades as you add more choices (the &quot;Lost in the Middle&quot; effect). A hierarchical system (Domain $\rightarrow$ Sub-domain $\rightarrow$ Task) maintains the same accuracy whether you have 10 tasks or 1,000."></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="efficiency: avoid llm for al" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="Build a Domain Dictionary. It is a simple CSV/JSON file:"><node TEXT="{&quot;Task_ID&quot;: &quot;PROMO_CHECK&quot;, &quot;Keywords&quot;: [&quot;coupon&quot;, &quot;discount&quot;, &quot;voucher&quot;, &quot;code&quot;], &quot;Regex&quot;: &quot;PROMO_\\d+&quot;}"></node></node><node TEXT="By running the user query through this dictionary using Fuzzy Match first, you will resolve 70% of intents in under 10ms without ever waking up an LLM."></node><node TEXT="The Hierarchical Architecture"><node TEXT="Think of this as a &quot;Waterfall&quot; design. Every query falls through these stages, stopping as soon as it finds a match."></node><node TEXT="Level 1: The Fast-Track (Code-Based)"><node TEXT="Purpose: Instant resolution of common patterns."></node><node TEXT="Logic: A collection of Regex and Lookup Tables."></node><node TEXT="Example: If input contains REQ-[0-9]+, it is immediately routed to the &quot;Order Status&quot; task."></node><node TEXT="LLM involvement: Zero."></node></node><node TEXT="Level 2: The Semantic Router (Embedding-Based)"><node TEXT="Purpose: Categorize the &quot;vibe&quot; of the query."></node><node TEXT="Logic: Map the query to a &quot;Domain&quot; (e.g., HR_Policy, Product_Specs, Account_Actions)."></node><node TEXT="Tool: Use a local vector store (FAISS) with a tiny embedding model."></node><node TEXT="LLM involvement: Zero (uses embeddings only)."></node></node><node TEXT="Level 3: The &quot;Specialist&quot; Agent (Task-Specific LLM)"><node TEXT="Purpose: Deep reasoning for a single domain."></node><node TEXT="Logic: Once Level 2 picks HR_Policy, you invoke a &quot;Mini-LLM&quot; (like GPT-4o-mini or a fine-tuned SLM) with a prompt specific only to HR."></node><node TEXT="LLM involvement: High (but narrow context)."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Managing 100 Tasks: The &quot;Manifest&quot; Design"><node TEXT="Task Registry"><node TEXT=""></node></node><node TEXT="How to manage it with an LLM:"><node TEXT="Selection: The LLM is shown only the descriptions of the top 5 candidates identified by the Semantic Router."></node><node TEXT="Mapping: The LLM picks the id and extracts the parameters."></node><node TEXT="Hydration: Your system pulls the full manifest for that id from your database to execute the RAG or API call."></node></node><node TEXT="Why this Design Wins"><node TEXT="Control over &quot;Context Contamination&quot;" FOLDED="true"><node TEXT="By isolating tasks, Task A’s documents never &quot;bleed&quot; into Task B’s answer. If a user asks about &quot;Refunds,&quot; the system physically cannot see &quot;Investment Strategy&quot; documents because the Intention Layer locked the Metadata Filter to doc_type: refund."></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Information Bottleneck&quot; (Input Control)"><node TEXT="Instead of letting the user&apos;s raw, messy text hit the LLM, you use your help tasks to squeeze the query into a Canonical Form."></node><node TEXT="The Problem"><node TEXT="User says: &quot;Hey, can you like, check if the 10% Summmer thingy is still active for my cart #8821? Thx.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="The Precision Fix"><node TEXT="Fuzzy/Typo: Summmer $\rightarrow$ SUMMER_10"></node><node TEXT="Regex: #8821 $\rightarrow$ order_id: 8821"></node><node TEXT="Normalization: Strips &quot;Hey,&quot; &quot;like,&quot; &quot;Thx.&quot;"></node></node></node><node TEXT="Resulting Prompt for LLM: Check validity of SUMMER_10 for order 8821."></node><node TEXT="Goodput Gain: The LLM doesn&apos;t have to waste &quot;attention tokens&quot; parsing slang or typos. It focuses 100% on the logic."></node></node><node TEXT="Task Injection (Dynamic Context)"><node TEXT="With 100 tasks, a &quot;Badput&quot; outcome is often caused by the LLM getting confused between Task 12 and Task 84."></node><node TEXT="The Precision Fix: Only inject the specific Task Manifest after your Semantic Router has narrowed the field."></node><node TEXT="Example: If the Semantic Router is 90% sure it’s a &quot;Policy RAG&quot; task, the prompt sent to the LLM only contains the instructions for Policy RAG."></node><node TEXT="Goodput Gain: By reducing the &quot;Reasoning Search Space,&quot; you virtually eliminate the chance of the LLM picking the wrong task."></node></node></node><node TEXT="Continuous Improvement (The &quot;Feedback Loop&quot;)"><node TEXT="Because the system is modular, you can log every time Level 3 (the LLM) had to correct Level 2 (the Router)."></node><node TEXT="If the Router frequently misses &quot;Promo&quot; questions, you don&apos;t rewrite the LLM prompt."></node><node TEXT="You simply add those missed questions as new &quot;Example Vectors&quot; in your Semantic Router."></node></node><node TEXT="&quot;Fail-Safe&quot; for 100+ Tasks"><node TEXT="When you reach 100 tasks, the biggest risk is the &quot;I don&apos;t know&quot; query. A modular system allows you to define a &quot;Fallback Task&quot; that triggers a general-purpose LLM agent to ask clarifying questions: &quot;I see you&apos;re asking about promotions, but are you trying to apply a code or check an expiration date?&quot;"></node></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="further improvement"><node TEXT="A. The &quot;Negative Centroid&quot; Strategy"><node TEXT="With 100 tasks, semantic &quot;collisions&quot; are your biggest enemy."></node><node TEXT="Improvement: For every task, define &quot;What I am NOT.&quot;"></node><node TEXT="Implementation: If a query is semantically close to both &quot;Refund&quot; and &quot;Status,&quot; the router checks a &quot;Negative Cluster.&quot; If the query matches the &quot;Negative Cluster&quot; of Task A (e.g., &quot;user is asking about money, not time&quot;), it forces the route to Task B."></node><node TEXT="How to build it (The &quot;Maker&quot; Way)"><node TEXT="Identify &quot;Conflict Pairs&quot;: Use a similarity matrix to see which of your 100 tasks have overlapping embeddings."></node><node TEXT="Collect &quot;Near-Misses&quot;: Look at your logs for queries that triggered Task A but were corrected to Task B."></node><node TEXT="Anchor the Error: Average those &quot;Near-Miss&quot; embeddings to create the $k^-$ for Task A."></node><node TEXT="Deploy as a Gate: Add this logic only to the &quot;High-Risk&quot; tasks."></node></node><node TEXT="Steering Formula:"><node TEXT=""></node></node></node><node TEXT="B. &quot;Just-in-Time&quot; Parameter Validation"><node TEXT="Improvement: Move validation inside the IRL, before the RAG/API call."></node><node TEXT="Implementation: If the IRL identifies the PROMO_CHECK task and extracts SUMMER_10, it should immediately ping a Bloom Filter or a local cache of valid codes."></node><node TEXT="Benefit: If the code is invalid, you return a &quot;Ghost Error&quot; (&quot;I see you&apos;re asking about SUMMER_10, but that code doesn&apos;t exist&quot;) without ever running the RAG or LLM."></node></node><node TEXT="C. Semantic Routing with &quot;Shadow&quot; Testing"><node TEXT="Improvement: Run a &quot;Champion-Challenger&quot; model for your router."></node><node TEXT="Implementation: While your Regex/Fuzzy logic (the Champion) handles the user, run a small SLM (the Challenger) in the background."></node><node TEXT="Benefit: Log whenever they disagree. This &quot;Disagreement Log&quot; becomes your primary dataset for fine-tuning the IRL."></node></node><node TEXT="Regex &quot;Greedy&quot; Problem"><node TEXT="explanation"><node TEXT="With 100+ tasks, Regex becomes a &quot;Double-Edged Sword.&quot; The biggest mistake is using Regex as a Hard Gate (If Match $\rightarrow$ Route). In a high-precision system, Regex should be a Featurizer, not a Router."></node><node TEXT="The Problem: Semantic Collisions"><node TEXT="If Task A is &quot;Check Balance&quot; and Task B is &quot;Check Promotion,&quot; and your Regex looks for check.*, you have a collision. If you add more specific rules, you end up with &quot;Regex Sprawl&quot;—a maintenance nightmare."></node></node></node><node TEXT="The Fix: Priority Tiers &amp; Functional Signals"><node TEXT="Functional vs. Domain Regex:"><node TEXT="Functional Regex: Detects actions (e.g., (show|get|list|check))."></node><node TEXT="Domain Regex: Detects entities (e.g., (balance|promo|transfer))."></node><node TEXT="The Logic: A match only counts if you have a [Function] + [Domain] pair. This reduces false positives by ~80%."></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Veto&quot; Regex (Negative Signal):"><node TEXT="Instead of matching what an intent is, use Regex to match what it cannot be."></node><node TEXT="Example: If the query contains &quot;apply for,&quot; it cannot be the &quot;Check Balance&quot; task, even if the word &quot;balance&quot; is present."></node></node><node TEXT="Regex as a &quot;Boost&quot; for Vector Search:"><node TEXT="Instead of routing via Regex, pass the Regex Match as a Metadata Flag to your Vector Search."></node><node TEXT="Logic: Score = CosineSim(query, task) + 0.2 * RegexMatch. This allows the &quot;Precision&quot; of Regex to assist the &quot;Fluidity&quot; of Semantic search without being brittle."></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="Multi-turn IRL"><node TEXT="The &quot;ReflectionGate&quot; Strategy (2026 Design Pattern)"><node TEXT="Instead of a raw list, use an Entropy-Filtered Episodic Memory."></node><node TEXT="Information Density Filtering:"><node TEXT="Don&apos;t store every turn. Only store turns that contain High Entropy (new entities or changed verbs)."></node><node TEXT="KISS Implementation: If a turn has no extracted entities and low semantic variance from the last turn, discard it from the &quot;Intention Context.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="The &quot;Slot Carryover&quot; Model:"><node TEXT="Instead of re-running intent for the whole history, carry over the Slot State."></node><node TEXT="Example"><node TEXT="Turn 1: &quot;Check balance for Account A.&quot; $\rightarrow$ (Intent: BAL, Slot: acc_id=A)"></node><node TEXT="Turn 2: &quot;What about Account B?&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="The IRL sees the new query, notices the verb is missing (&quot;What about...&quot;), and &quot;Hydrates&quot; the query using the previous intent: &quot;Check balance for Account B.&quot;"></node></node><node TEXT="Implicit vs. Explicit State:"><node TEXT="Implicit: Use the last 3 embeddings (averaged or weighted)."></node><node TEXT="Explicit: Use a &quot;State Machine&quot; where certain intents lock the context. (e.g., If the user is in the &quot;Transfer Funds&quot; flow, the IRL assumes any 3-word query is related to that flow until a &quot;Clear Context&quot; signal is detected)."></node></node></node><node TEXT=""></node></node></node></node></node><node TEXT="second step"><node TEXT="features"><node TEXT="100+ subtasks"></node></node></node></node>
</map>